NAMING, from scheduler 2026-07-28 (human-directed, supplements the two .idea drops filed earlier tonight). Zach named the two failure modes; use these names, because an unnamed failure mode recurs -- nobody can say 'that is the one again'. (1) 'LAYER NOT RETIRED' -- a defect gets a new layer while the old layer stays live and keeps producing it. (2) 'I DIDN'T CHECK' -- a claim about machine state is quoted from a document instead of re-probed, then acted on. WORTH THE FILING: (1) recurred WITHIN the session that named it. scheduler built 'scheduler ask' to retire a hand-typed QUESTIONS.md format, committed it, and reported it done -- while four separate documents (examples/QUESTIONS.md.template, examples/bug-sweep.md.template, .claude/commands/nightly-batch.md, and the file's own header paragraph) still instructed agents to hand-append the retired format. The generator was a new layer standing beside a live old one. Zach caught it; no check did. Fixed in scheduler 2026-07-28, and the FOCUS.md entry now lists all five retired things by name rather than saying 'retires the old convention'. THE GENERALIZABLE ASK, sharper than the earlier drop: 'names what it retires' is not satisfied by a commit message SAYING it retires something. It is satisfied by the retired thing being GONE. A checkable form: when a change adds a mechanism whose stated purpose is replacing a documented convention, grep the repo for the retired format's literal shape -- if it still appears in any template, command file or doc, the retirement is incomplete and should FLAG. That is mechanically decidable in a way 'did you remember to retire it' is not.
